[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m but `c_src/src/lib.c` *defines*:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int arity(unsigned char len, int *params) { if (len < 2) return -1; ... }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A caller compiled against the header passes a full 32-bit `int` in `%edi`. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m callee, disassembled from the built `.so`, truncates it:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1634:  mov    %edi,%eax
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 163a:  mov    %al,-0x4(%rbp)     <-- keeps only the low 8 bits
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 163d:  cmpb   $0x1,-0x4(%rbp)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1641:  ja     164a               <-- unsigned comparison
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So the observable contract at the `.so` boundary is `len & 0xFF`, compared
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *unsigned*. The Rust export takes `c_int` (matching the public header) and masks
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to 8 bits, reproducing this exactly — e.g. `len = 256` and `len = -256` both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m truncate to 0 and return `-1`, while `len = -1` truncates to 255 and takes the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `arity4` branch. Verified in Phase C.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### `compare_allocations` — genuinely allocator-dependent
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C compares the *addresses* returned by two `malloc` calls:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if (ptr1 < ptr2) result = 1; else if (ptr1 > ptr2) result = 2; else result = 3;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Because glibc's tcache is LIFO, `malloc,malloc,free,free` reverses the order of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the two chunks, so consecutive calls return `1,2,1,2,...` (+10 when `val1 > 0`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is process-global state **shared** by the C `.so` and the Rust `.so`, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m call the same libc `malloc`. The Rust translation is therefore faithful by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m construction — it performs the same two `malloc`s and the same two `free`s in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the same order — but a naive "call C once, call Rust once" test is guaranteed to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m diverge simply because the second call observes a flipped heap.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The tests handle this by comparing **phase-neutral call pairs**: each side is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m called twice in a row and the two-element result *sequences* are compared. Two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m calls restore the tcache to its entry state, so the pattern is self-resetting
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and independent of the starting parity. This is strictly stronger than a single
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m call — it observes *both* heap phases per input. Applies to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `compare_allocations` and to everything that transitively calls it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`arity4`, `arity3`, `arity2`, `arity`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Feature combinations
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/Cargo.toml` declares **no `[features]` table** and no optional
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m dependencies, so the default (and only) feature combination is the empty set.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cargo test --no-default-features` is equivalent to `cargo test`. Both are run
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in Phase D for completeness.
