[38;5;252m[1m## Two findings worth your attention[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10marity[0m has a header/definition mismatch in the C.[22m [38;5;10mlib.h[0m declares [38;5;10mint arity(int len, ...)[0m but [38;5;10mlib.c[0m defines [38;5;10munsigned char len[0m. I disassembled the built [38;5;10m.so[0m and confirmed GCC truncates to the low byte and compares [3munsigned[23m — so [38;5;10marity(256, ...)[0m returns [38;5;10m-1[0m, and [38;5;10marity(-1, ...)[0m dispatches to [38;5;10marity4[0m. The Rust masks to 8 bits, which matches. A translation that read the full [38;5;10mint[0m would crash on [38;5;10marity(256, NULL)[0m; that mutant is one of the ones the suite kills.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mcompare_allocations[0m is genuinely environment-dependent[22m, and this is what most of the effort went into. It compares two raw [38;5;10mmalloc[0m addresses, so its result depends on glibc's tcache LIFO ordering (alternating 1↔2) — process-global state [3mshared[23m by both [38;5;10m.so[0ms, since both call the same libc. Naively calling C once then Rust once diverges every time, even though both are correct. Worse, under [38;5;10mcargo test[0m's parallel threads, glibc's [38;5;10marena_get[0m falls back to a different arena under contention and the ordering stops alternating at all, which produced flaky failures that were [1mnot[22m translation bugs. The harness now serializes these measurements, measures both sides adjacently so their ordered pairs are comparable, and only reports a divergence once the C side reproduces its own result.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Why I trust the passing result[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Passing tests prove nothing unless they can fail, so I injected 33 mutants into the Rust and reran the suite. 29 were killed. The 4 survivors are each provably equivalent to the original — e.g. the [38;5;10mif (*str)[0m guard in [38;5;10mprocess_string[0m is redundant because [38;5;10mstrlen("")[0m is already 0, and the [38;5;10mresult = 3[0m tie branch is unreachable since two live allocations can't share an address.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This process caught two real weaknesses in my own harness that would otherwise have produced a false pass: comparing allocator phases as an unordered set let the swapped-[38;5;10m<[0m/[38;5;10m>[0m mutant survive (fixed by ordered comparison), and my initial diagnostic was itself perturbing the heap because [38;5;10mcollect()[0m allocates into the same 32-byte malloc bin as [38;5;10mmalloc(4)[0m.[0m[0m
